#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8c898fc-6802-5296-9866-c844f9ed0349" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Boundary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab94733-94c4-5ade-b091-2f1f3e93b7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Other" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149813} {:answer/id #uuid "1fffd8ad-b10b-59c8-959a-10bd4ee97f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149814} {:answer/id #uuid "27aca700-68e9-5080-8f15-a748b43659aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149815} {:answer/id #uuid "70f1be98-cd58-5a9c-ae9b-9065ceaa6eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149816} {:answer/id #uuid "73fb2e77-8554-5281-bffc-4a1182a0ffc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27aca700-68e9-5080-8f15-a748b43659aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149815} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb448d18-8be8-5341-814e-852bd03ff01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b10fb07-f2e4-5043-9db4-94a7330fcd8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338513" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=0$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nThese are Dirichlet boundary conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "944bf8ed-46b7-55b7-9ae7-f3686f03d94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149819} {:answer/id #uuid "3b51d554-2a7e-5113-a1a9-745a3657fa28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b51d554-2a7e-5113-a1a9-745a3657fa28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149820} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f42c2ad-4295-5c76-9034-69c1ed9b17f2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4d8744-68d8-54ae-a159-8c21bede4028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149822} {:answer/id #uuid "494865e3-dfc1-541f-9ff5-5d9f025d0fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4d8744-68d8-54ae-a159-8c21bede4028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149822} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f4839c-09e1-50b0-aed6-3bdd704ddc14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da9aa752-15cc-53a4-9162-21052fdd6da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149825} {:answer/id #uuid "414fd097-359f-555a-a1cb-cbb37664e91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9aa752-15cc-53a4-9162-21052fdd6da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149825} :answer-field/id #uuid "73fc3e34-e0bc-5850-b9c8-10f5bb3ae547" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2443c50-f862-5df4-9df6-1d1704f55fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149828} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7bf673-832e-554f-abad-c9c2bf816133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149829} {:answer/id #uuid "57499eb1-9f19-50a3-b6ff-7b2d72e110f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2443c50-f862-5df4-9df6-1d1704f55fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0cd74a7-381b-5ae9-b084-02522f2dcd22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b711ebb4-4d84-521d-8e27-4cb308558d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338516" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8c898fc-6802-5296-9866-c844f9ed0349" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Boundary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632ad8cf-b54f-55aa-a1aa-186d3ca82abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Other" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363200} {:answer/id #uuid "7eab5691-01e3-50b8-9d2b-2ed48d1f8747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363201} {:answer/id #uuid "66dfed4b-8930-534e-b251-7a9023ac8f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363202} {:answer/id #uuid "e0064102-c08e-588e-a35d-d5f5d01932c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363203} {:answer/id #uuid "facf965b-3243-5a8d-8251-24b574016444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eab5691-01e3-50b8-9d2b-2ed48d1f8747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363201} :answer-field/id #uuid "7623e14c-5206-5bd0-a5da-344350db9827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b670b9b-dff6-597e-8aac-8d40070f8fbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-338618" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1,\\,-2\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=-2$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\n2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938283d2-beda-511f-aecc-58595635904b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149832} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9dabe7-7b7f-58e1-8fc8-b3ce980ec136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "938283d2-beda-511f-aecc-58595635904b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149832} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c80d90-6494-52f2-89ea-dc293115cce6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4ccee7-d559-5af5-b30f-9e04099e88ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149835} {:answer/id #uuid "50336b90-4562-50ac-ac48-fb5939390087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50336b90-4562-50ac-ac48-fb5939390087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149836} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e4c0a6-b020-5b0e-8fcc-bd3c466e555a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8c4ea9-94a2-53d0-b8a3-8fda9ee2ab65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149838} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9f7035-028a-5142-a75c-8e88efb39abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149839} {:answer/id #uuid "3405b581-806b-53a8-a6a5-dd21443765a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8c4ea9-94a2-53d0-b8a3-8fda9ee2ab65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149838} :answer-field/id #uuid "1994c3e7-0d94-55f9-be69-ea8b4571bc92" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149837} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "699220fe-e38d-5f08-9f80-5de48771711e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149842} {:answer/id #uuid "7bffb848-97bb-50e8-b92a-2978c719ac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bffb848-97bb-50e8-b92a-2978c719ac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a52a3b-0950-57fc-86c1-cd6057ec4814" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc19be1d-a106-5173-ab6a-0a0d552a59ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-338646" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea5460-9fce-5292-8a2c-a5d61a66b74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363207} {:answer/id #uuid "71513aac-53cc-5e33-81cb-39b4047cef69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eea5460-9fce-5292-8a2c-a5d61a66b74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363207} :answer-field/id #uuid "5893df9b-3f00-5fcc-a94f-3423a92dbaeb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363206} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34aa6b8-a20f-5a9f-a816-56d3681f98ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363210} {:answer/id #uuid "3a56026b-35fa-55a0-be5b-45979c6d3b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363211} {:answer/id #uuid "2469e65b-db6c-5ae7-889d-ec2871a9671e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2469e65b-db6c-5ae7-889d-ec2871a9671e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363212} :answer-field/id #uuid "21c8ee33-e7a4-5857-a896-6cb023a28d9c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363209} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e242363-7d99-56c3-b94a-6550793c97a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363214} {:answer/id #uuid "f4078ed4-ddcf-551e-b97a-37de52c6a121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e242363-7d99-56c3-b94a-6550793c97a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363214} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e1416b-f4bd-5da5-af6e-a4897604aac4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29df197-9fa6-5f41-befe-681aa4a09b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363217} {:answer/id #uuid "02deb03c-830e-52ce-85c0-052801a33aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a29df197-9fa6-5f41-befe-681aa4a09b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363217} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8260649-8060-5aae-8116-a1e3b9b812d5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a8c335f-0456-5a5d-854f-e83759169ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-338695" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$"}]}
